.TH DANBY-PACKET 1
.SH NAME
danby-packet \- a free particle, spreading
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B danby/packet
.SH MODEL
Every other quantum model here draws a standing shape.
.IR Danby-schrodinger (1),
.IR danby-hydrogen (1)
and
.IR danby-bands (1)
all solve for states that do not change, and
.IR danby-tunnel (1)
is a steady beam.  This one is the exception, and that is why it is
worth having: it is the only one in which a wave function actually moves
and changes, and the diagram draws it doing so.
.PP
A free Gaussian packet, released at the origin with momentum k.  Its
centre travels at the group velocity k/m \(em the classical answer, and
the only classical thing about it.  Its width does not stay put:
.PP
.EX
sigma(t) = sqrt( sigma0^2 + ( t / 2 m sigma0 )^2 )
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Danby/packet
is a
.IR danbylab (2)
script \(em the whole model is the text of
.BR /appl/danby/packet.spec .
.SS What to look for
.B It spreads, and it never unspreads.
The width has no term that can decrease.  A free particle released
anywhere becomes less and less localised for ever, and nothing in the
equations offers a way back.
.PP
.B A tighter packet spreads faster.
.I Sigma0
appears in the DENOMINATOR of the growing term, so squeezing the initial
packet makes it fly apart sooner.  Halve the initial width and it is
wider than the loose one within a few units of time.  That is the
uncertainty principle stated about time rather than about measurement:
pin a particle down and it escapes you the quicker.
.PP
.B The momentum spread never changes.
Nothing acts on a free particle, so dp stays at 1/(2 sigma0) for ever
and the product is
.PP
.EX
dx dp = sigma(t) / 2 sigma0
.EE
.PP
which is exactly 1/2 at release.  That is the least any state can have,
and it is why a Gaussian and no other shape is where textbooks start.
From then on it only grows.
.SS The diagram
The point of the model.  It samples |psi|^2 at forty-eight fixed
positions and draws the curve through them, so the bump visibly slides
right and flattens as the run goes on.
.PP
Because the sample positions are fixed and the frame is pinned, what
moves on the screen is the packet and not the view rescaling around it.
The curve is normalised, so as it widens it must also fall \(em the area
under it is a probability and stays one.
.IR Danbylabtest (1)
checks exactly that, by summing the drawn samples.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/danby/packet.spec
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR danby (1),
.IR danby-schrodinger (1),
.IR danby-tunnel (1),
.IR danbylab (2)
.SH BUGS
The spreading is the closed-form answer substituted in, not a solution
being integrated.  Nothing here solves the time-dependent Schrodinger
equation; the packet is told how wide to be at each instant.  A real
solver \(em the packet meeting a barrier and splitting into a reflected
and a transmitted piece, which is the picture everyone wants \(em is a
PDE, and
.IR danbylab (2)
has no family that can host one.
.PP
Only the envelope is drawn.  A real packet carries
.I exp(ikx)
underneath, oscillating far faster than the envelope, and |psi|^2 hides
it completely.  Nothing on screen shows the momentum except the speed at
which the bump travels.
.PP
The drawn window runs from \-4 to 20.  Late in a fast run the packet has
spread past the right edge, and the sum of the samples then falls short
of one \(em honestly, because the probability really has left the
window, but it means the normalisation check only holds early and the
test only asks it early.
.PP
.IR Danbylabtest (1)
checks the minimum-uncertainty value at release, sigma against its
closed form at five later times, the group velocity, that the drawn
density integrates to one, and that a tighter packet overtakes a looser
one.  The release check is exact and blind \(em at t = 0 the whole
spreading term is zero, so it sees neither the mass nor the initial
width in it \(em which is why the later times are there.  The
normalisation check is the only one that sees the peak factor at all:
breaking root-two-pi to 2 passes every other test in the file.
